steps.body:
A view doesn't own its characters, so it's only good while their owner is alive and unchanged. Nothing checks that. Each of these compiles on g++ 10.2 without a warning, even with `-Wall -Wextra`, and leaves a dangling view that's undefined behavior to read:

```cpp norun
std::string_view sym = std::string("AAPL") + ".O";  // the temporary string dies at the ;

std::string_view venue(const std::string& s) {      // fine on its own...
    return std::string_view(s).substr(5);
}
std::string_view v = venue(load_symbol());           // ...but load_symbol()'s string dies at the ;

std::string_view first_field() {
    std::string line = read_line();
    return line;                                     // line dies as the function returns
}
```

Two quieter ways: a view into a `std::string` that then grows, since `+=` can move its characters to a bigger buffer, and a view into a string inside a `std::vector` that grows. A short string's characters live inside the string object, so when the vector moves its elements, the characters move too.

The rules that keep views safe:

- Take views as parameters: the caller's text outlives the call.
- Return a view only into text the caller passed in, or into a literal, which lives for the whole program.
- Keep views into a buffer only while it's alive and unchanged, like the fields of the message you're handling now. Copy whatever you keep with `std::string(view)`, a conversion C++ makes you write out.
- `view.data()` isn't followed by a `'\0'`, so never hand it to a C function that expects one, like `atoi` or `printf("%s")`.

On your Mac, `clang++ -std=c++17` warns about the first and third cases by default (`-Wdangling-gsl` and `-Wreturn-stack-address`), and building with `-fsanitize=address` reports the second as a `heap-use-after-free` when it runs. g++ on Linux takes the same `-fsanitize=address`, and MSVC has `/fsanitize=address`.

