steps.body:
`std::stoi` needs a `std::string` (a copy, if you start from a view), skips leading spaces, throws on bad input, and quietly reads `"12abc"` as 12. `std::from_chars` from `<charconv>` is made for parsers: it reads digits from a range of chars, never allocates, never throws, ignores the locale, and tells you where it stopped.

```cpp
std::string_view text = "19275|38=100";
std::int64_t ticks = 0;
const auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(text.data(), text.data() + text.size(), ticks);
std::cout << ticks << ' ' << (ec == std::errc{}) << ' ' << (ptr - text.data()) << '\n';  // 19275 1 5
```

It returns a `std::from_chars_result`, two members that structured bindings unpack:

- `ec` is `std::errc{}` on success, `std::errc::invalid_argument` when there's no digit to start with, and `std::errc::result_out_of_range` when the number doesn't fit the type. On failure the value is left as it was.
- `ptr` points just past the last digit read. To require that a whole field is a number, check `ptr == last`.

It accepts a leading `-` for signed types, but no spaces and no `+`, which suits a wire format: anything unexpected is an error. Parsing 200,000 numbers already in memory took about 9 ms here with `std::istringstream`, 9 ms with `substr` and `std::stoll`, and under 4 ms with `from_chars`, rough times from the emulated sandbox.

g++ 10.2 has `from_chars` for integers only. The `double` version arrived in GCC 11, so here it doesn't compile:

```cpp error
#include <charconv>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const char text[] = "192.75";
    double price = 0;
    std::from_chars(text, text + 6, price);  // g++ 10: no matching function for call to 'from_chars'
    std::cout << price << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

That costs a gateway little. The messages in this lesson carry prices as integer ticks, `44=19275` for $192.75 in cents, the way binary feeds like Nasdaq's ITCH send integer prices with implied decimals. Real FIX sends `44=192.75` as text, and the example below turns that into ticks with two integer parses. So this lesson parses prices into integer ticks rather than into the `double` fields of `Tick`, and the later lesson on numbers for money shows why trading code keeps prices in ticks even where parsing a `double` is easy. Apple clang 21 on your Mac does have the `double` version, so code that uses it builds there and fails on g++ 10.

